<p>In Part A, I used a pretrained flow model to denoise, generate, and edit images. In Part B, I built UNets and trained them on MNIST, first for single-step denoising and then for flow matching.</p>
<h1 class="report-part" id="part-a">Part A: Exploring the power of flow models</h1>
<p>I used PixNerd with 512 × 512 inputs and RGB values in [−1, 1]. Unless stated otherwise, I used the prompt <code>"a high quality photo"</code> and Euler step size 0.02. For classifier-free guidance, I used w = 7 and an empty prompt for the unconditional prediction.</p>
<h2 id="part-a-1">1. Visualizing training and sampling</h2>
<section id="part-a-1-1"><h3>1.1 Constructing noisy training inputs</h3>
<p>I implemented the interpolation function and used my portrait with the supplied experiment loop, which reuses one Gaussian noise tensor ε for all three times. The interpolation is xₜ = (1 − t)ε + tx₁, where x₁ is the clean image. Increasing t keeps more of the portrait. The supplied endpoint checks passed: t = 0 returns the noise and t = 1 returns the original image.</p>
<figure class=""><a href="/images/cs180/project-3/a/training_inputs.png"><img src="/images/cs180/project-3/a/training_inputs.png" width="1760" height="440" loading="lazy" alt="Original portrait and noisy inputs at t = 0.25, 0.50, and 0.75."></a><figcaption>Original portrait and noisy inputs at t = 0.25, 0.50, and 0.75.</figcaption></figure>
</section>
<section id="part-a-1-2"><h3>1.2 Classical denoising</h3>
<p>The supplied Gaussian baseline divides each noisy input by t to restore the image amplitude, then applies Gaussian blur. It tests σ = 1, 2, 4, 8, and 16 with kernel width 6σ + 1 and selects the lowest mean squared error (MSE) against the original. The selected σ values are 8, 4, and 2 at t = 0.25, 0.50, and 0.75. Blur reduces grain but loses facial detail, especially at t = 0.25.</p>
<figure class=""><a href="/images/cs180/project-3/a/blur_0.25.png"><img src="/images/cs180/project-3/a/blur_0.25.png" width="880" height="440" loading="lazy" alt="Noisy input and Gaussian baseline at t = 0.25."></a><figcaption>Noisy input and Gaussian baseline at t = 0.25.</figcaption></figure>
<figure class=""><a href="/images/cs180/project-3/a/blur_0.5.png"><img src="/images/cs180/project-3/a/blur_0.5.png" width="880" height="440" loading="lazy" alt="Noisy input and Gaussian baseline at t = 0.50."></a><figcaption>Noisy input and Gaussian baseline at t = 0.50.</figcaption></figure>
<figure class=""><a href="/images/cs180/project-3/a/blur_0.75.png"><img src="/images/cs180/project-3/a/blur_0.75.png" width="880" height="440" loading="lazy" alt="Noisy input and Gaussian baseline at t = 0.75."></a><figcaption>Noisy input and Gaussian baseline at t = 0.75.</figcaption></figure>
</section>
<section id="part-a-1-3"><h3>1.3 One-step denoising</h3>
<p>I predicted the velocity once and estimated the clean endpoint with x̂₁ = xₜ + (1 − t)vθ(xₜ, t, c), where c is the photo prompt embedding. The portrait is much clearer than the Gaussian baseline. At t = 0.25, the estimate smooths the face and background more than at t = 0.75.</p>
<figure class=""><a href="/images/cs180/project-3/a/one_step_0.25.png"><img src="/images/cs180/project-3/a/one_step_0.25.png" width="1320" height="440" loading="lazy" alt="Original, noisy input, and one-step estimate at t = 0.25."></a><figcaption>Original, noisy input, and one-step estimate at t = 0.25.</figcaption></figure>
<figure class=""><a href="/images/cs180/project-3/a/one_step_0.5.png"><img src="/images/cs180/project-3/a/one_step_0.5.png" width="1320" height="440" loading="lazy" alt="Original, noisy input, and one-step estimate at t = 0.50."></a><figcaption>Original, noisy input, and one-step estimate at t = 0.50.</figcaption></figure>
<figure class=""><a href="/images/cs180/project-3/a/one_step_0.75.png"><img src="/images/cs180/project-3/a/one_step_0.75.png" width="1320" height="440" loading="lazy" alt="Original, noisy input, and one-step estimate at t = 0.75."></a><figcaption>Original, noisy input, and one-step estimate at t = 0.75.</figcaption></figure>
</section>
<section id="part-a-1-4"><h3>1.4 Visualizing velocity</h3>
<p>The exact velocity for this image/noise pair is x₁ − ε. I compared the predicted update (1 − t)vθ with the ground-truth update (1 − t)(x₁ − ε). For each pixel, I squared their difference, averaged over RGB, and took the square root before any display scaling. The supplied plotting code displays all updates on one symmetric RGB scale, with zero at middle gray. All error maps share one linear scale from zero to the largest error across the three times.</p>
<div class="report-grid report-cols-1">
<figure class=""><a href="/images/cs180/project-3/a/velocity_original.png"><img src="/images/cs180/project-3/a/velocity_original.png" width="512" height="512" loading="lazy" alt="Original portrait used for the velocity comparison."></a><figcaption>Original portrait used for the velocity comparison.</figcaption></figure>
</div>
<figure class=""><a href="/images/cs180/project-3/a/velocity_0.25.png"><img src="/images/cs180/project-3/a/velocity_0.25.png" width="1760" height="440" loading="lazy" alt="t = 0.25: noisy image, predicted update, ground-truth update, error heatmap."></a><figcaption>t = 0.25: noisy image, predicted update, ground-truth update, error heatmap.</figcaption></figure>
<figure class=""><a href="/images/cs180/project-3/a/velocity_0.50.png"><img src="/images/cs180/project-3/a/velocity_0.50.png" width="1760" height="440" loading="lazy" alt="t = 0.50: noisy image, predicted update, ground-truth update, error heatmap."></a><figcaption>t = 0.50: noisy image, predicted update, ground-truth update, error heatmap.</figcaption></figure>
<figure class=""><a href="/images/cs180/project-3/a/velocity_0.75.png"><img src="/images/cs180/project-3/a/velocity_0.75.png" width="1760" height="440" loading="lazy" alt="t = 0.75: noisy image, predicted update, ground-truth update, error heatmap."></a><figcaption>t = 0.75: noisy image, predicted update, ground-truth update, error heatmap.</figcaption></figure>
<p>The error maps are brighter and more structured at t = 0.25. Errors remain around the face and background at t = 0.50, while the t = 0.75 map is much darker. More image information and a smaller remaining update make the later-time estimate closer to the known target.</p>
</section>
<section id="part-a-1-5"><h3>1.5 Iterative denoising with Euler sampling</h3>
<p>My sampler recomputes velocity after each update x ← x + hvθ(x, t, c). It shortens the last step to reach t = 1 exactly. From the saved t = 0.50 input, h = 0.02 gives 25 updates.</p>
<figure class="p3-trajectory"><a href="/images/cs180/project-3/a/trajectory-every-fifth.png"><img src="/images/cs180/project-3/a/trajectory-every-fifth.png" width="1320" height="880" loading="lazy" alt="Euler trajectory: initial noisy image at t = 0.50, then every fifth update at t = 0.60, 0.70, 0.80, 0.90, and 1.00."></a><figcaption>Euler trajectory: initial noisy image at t = 0.50, then every fifth update at t = 0.60, 0.70, 0.80, 0.90, and 1.00.</figcaption></figure>
<figure class=""><a href="/images/cs180/project-3/a/comparison.png"><img src="/images/cs180/project-3/a/comparison.png" width="1760" height="440" loading="lazy" alt="Same t = 0.50 input: original, Gaussian blur, one-step estimate, and Euler result."></a><figcaption>Same t = 0.50 input: original, Gaussian blur, one-step estimate, and Euler result.</figcaption></figure>
<p>Euler sampling restores a clear portrait, but it does not have the lowest pixel error in this example. The saved reconstruction MSE is 0.00926410 for blur, 0.00212908 for one step, and 0.00250297 for Euler. Both learned methods preserve more detail than blur; one step stays slightly closer to the original by this metric.</p>
</section>
<section id="part-a-1-6"><h3>1.6 Generating images from noise</h3>
<p>I started from five independent Gaussian tensors at t = 0 and used 50 Euler updates. Seeds 180–184 are shown in order. The generic photo prompt produces people in all five samples. Some faces and bodies are distorted; seed 183 has a particularly rough, painterly appearance.</p>
