<h1 class="report-part" id="part-b">Part B: Flow matching from scratch!</h1>
<p>The skeleton supplied the dataset and plotting helpers and the flow-matching wrapper classes. I implemented the network blocks, forward passes, losses, samplers, and experiment code.</p>
<p>I trained on the 60,000 MNIST training images and used the 10,000-image test split for denoising examples. Pixels are in [0, 1]. Training batches are shuffled, with fresh noise drawn after each batch is fetched. The seed is 180. The loss plots show per-step MSE and a 100-step moving average.</p>
<h2 id="part-b-1">1. Training a single-step denoising UNet</h2>
<section id="part-b-1-1"><h3>1.1 Implementing the UNet</h3>
<p>I use D for the base number of hidden feature channels. I built convolution blocks from 3 × 3 convolutions, batch normalization, and GELU. Stride-2 convolutions reduce 28 × 28 inputs to 14 × 14 and then 7 × 7. Average pooling compresses the 7 × 7 features to 1 × 1; a transposed convolution expands them again. Upsampling blocks use transposed convolutions and concatenate features from matching encoder resolutions. A final convolution predicts one value per pixel with no output activation.</p>
</section>
<section id="part-b-1-2"><h3>1.2 Using the UNet to train a denoiser</h3>
<p>For five fixed training digits, I reused each digit’s noise pattern and formed z = x + σε at σ = 0.0, 0.2, 0.4, 0.5, 0.6, 0.8, and 1.0. This adds noise without scaling down the clean digit.</p>
<figure class=""><a href="/images/cs180/project-3/b/noising_process.png"><img src="/images/cs180/project-3/b/noising_process.png" width="1440" height="690" loading="lazy" alt="Five digits, one per row, with σ increasing across columns."></a><figcaption>Five digits, one per row, with σ increasing across columns.</figcaption></figure>
</section>
<section id="part-b-1-2-1"><h4>1.2.1 Training</h4>
<p>I completed the supplied training loop with fresh noisy inputs and model predictions, and added evaluation figures and checkpoint saving. I trained a D = 128 UNet for five epochs with batch size 256, Adam learning rate 0.0001, and σ = 0.5. The MSE target is the clean image. I kept ten test digits and their noise fixed across the epoch comparisons. Mean training loss falls from 0.020177 in epoch 1 to 0.008300 in epoch 5.</p>
<figure class=""><a href="/images/cs180/project-3/b/single_step_denoiser_loss.png"><img src="/images/cs180/project-3/b/single_step_denoiser_loss.png" width="960" height="360" loading="lazy" alt="Single-step denoising training loss over all five epochs."></a><figcaption>Single-step denoising training loss over all five epochs.</figcaption></figure>
<figure class=""><a href="/images/cs180/project-3/b/denoiser_epoch_1.png"><img src="/images/cs180/project-3/b/denoiser_epoch_1.png" width="1440" height="413" loading="lazy" alt="Epoch 1: clean test digits, noisy inputs, and denoised predictions."></a><figcaption>Epoch 1: clean test digits, noisy inputs, and denoised predictions.</figcaption></figure>
<figure class=""><a href="/images/cs180/project-3/b/denoiser_epoch_5.png"><img src="/images/cs180/project-3/b/denoiser_epoch_5.png" width="1440" height="413" loading="lazy" alt="Epoch 5: clean test digits, noisy inputs, and denoised predictions."></a><figcaption>Epoch 5: clean test digits, noisy inputs, and denoised predictions.</figcaption></figure>
<p>The epoch 1 predictions already recover most digit shapes. At epoch 5, strokes are cleaner and small breaks are reduced. Some fine features are still smoothed.</p>
</section>
<section id="part-b-1-2-2"><h4>1.2.2 Out-of-distribution testing</h4>
<p>I kept the same test digit and Gaussian noise pattern and varied σ across the seven levels. The model was trained only at σ = 0.5. It retains the 7 at lower noise levels, but the stroke becomes less stable at σ = 0.8 and 1.0. The highest-noise output has extra marks and a less clear shape.</p>
<figure class=""><a href="/images/cs180/project-3/b/ood_denoising.png"><img src="/images/cs180/project-3/b/ood_denoising.png" width="1440" height="413" loading="lazy" alt="Same test 7 at σ = 0.0, 0.2, 0.4, 0.5, 0.6, 0.8, and 1.0. Clean, noisy, and denoised rows."></a><figcaption>Same test 7 at σ = 0.0, 0.2, 0.4, 0.5, 0.6, 0.8, and 1.0. Clean, noisy, and denoised rows.</figcaption></figure>
</section>
<section id="part-b-1-2-3"><h4>1.2.3 Denoising pure noise</h4>
<p>I trained a fresh D = 128 UNet for five epochs with the same batch size, Adam learning rate, and MSE objective, but gave it independent Gaussian noise as input and a clean digit as target. The ten evaluation noise inputs stay fixed. Mean training loss is 0.073273 in epoch 1 and 0.067408 in epoch 5.</p>
<figure class=""><a href="/images/cs180/project-3/b/pure_noise_denoiser_loss.png"><img src="/images/cs180/project-3/b/pure_noise_denoiser_loss.png" width="960" height="360" loading="lazy" alt="Pure-noise denoiser training loss."></a><figcaption>Pure-noise denoiser training loss.</figcaption></figure>
<figure class=""><a href="/images/cs180/project-3/b/pure_noise_epoch_1.png"><img src="/images/cs180/project-3/b/pure_noise_epoch_1.png" width="1440" height="276" loading="lazy" alt="Epoch 1: fixed noise inputs above and predictions below."></a><figcaption>Epoch 1: fixed noise inputs above and predictions below.</figcaption></figure>
<figure class=""><a href="/images/cs180/project-3/b/pure_noise_epoch_5.png"><img src="/images/cs180/project-3/b/pure_noise_epoch_5.png" width="1440" height="276" loading="lazy" alt="Epoch 5: fixed noise inputs above and predictions below."></a><figcaption>Epoch 5: fixed noise inputs above and predictions below.</figcaption></figure>
<p>Different noise inputs produce nearly identical blurry outputs. Their bright centers and overlapping curves resemble an average of the training digits. Since the input noise is independent of the target, it gives the model no information about which digit to predict. Under MSE, predicting the conditional mean minimizes expected error; here that mean is the average training image. A lower loss therefore does not imply varied, recognizable generated digits.</p>
</section>
<h2 id="part-b-2">2. Training a flow matching model</h2>
<section id="part-b-2-1"><h3>2.1 Adding time conditioning to UNet</h3>
<p>I added two FCBlocks, each with Linear → GELU → Linear, to embed normalized time. Their outputs multiply features after unflattening and after the first upsampling block.</p>
</section>
<section id="part-b-2-2"><h3>2.2 Training the UNet</h3>
<p>For each training image x₁, I sample Gaussian x₀ and t uniformly in [0, 1], form xₜ = (1 − t)x₀ + tx₁, and train the UNet to predict x₁ − x₀ using MSE.</p>
<p>This D = 64 model uses batch size 64 and Adam with initial learning rate 0.01 for ten epochs. After each epoch, the exponential scheduler multiplies the learning rate by 0.1^(1/10), about 0.7943. Mean training loss decreases from 0.185022 in epoch 1 to 0.093514 in epoch 10.</p>
<figure class=""><a href="/images/cs180/project-3/b/time_conditioned_fm_loss.png"><img src="/images/cs180/project-3/b/time_conditioned_fm_loss.png" width="960" height="360" loading="lazy" alt="Time-conditioned flow-matching training loss."></a><figcaption>Time-conditioned flow-matching training loss.</figcaption></figure>
</section>
<section id="part-b-2-3"><h3>2.3 Sampling from the UNet</h3>
<p>Sampling uses 50 Euler steps of size 0.02 from pure noise. I reuse seeds 180–189 at all three checkpoints.</p>
<figure class=""><a href="/images/cs180/project-3/b/time_fm_samples_epoch_1.png"><img src="/images/cs180/project-3/b/time_fm_samples_epoch_1.png" width="1440" height="240" loading="lazy" alt="Time-conditioned samples after epoch 1, seeds 180–189."></a><figcaption>Time-conditioned samples after epoch 1, seeds 180–189.</figcaption></figure>
<figure class=""><a href="/images/cs180/project-3/b/time_fm_samples_epoch_5.png"><img src="/images/cs180/project-3/b/time_fm_samples_epoch_5.png" width="1440" height="240" loading="lazy" alt="Time-conditioned samples after epoch 5, seeds 180–189."></a><figcaption>Time-conditioned samples after epoch 5, seeds 180–189.</figcaption></figure>
<figure class=""><a href="/images/cs180/project-3/b/time_fm_samples_epoch_10.png"><img src="/images/cs180/project-3/b/time_fm_samples_epoch_10.png" width="1440" height="240" loading="lazy" alt="Time-conditioned samples after epoch 10, seeds 180–189."></a><figcaption>Time-conditioned samples after epoch 10, seeds 180–189.</figcaption></figure>
<p>Epoch 1 contains scattered strokes and ambiguous shapes. By epochs 5 and 10, more outputs resemble digits, including several clear 3, 6, 7, and 9 shapes. Some epoch 10 outputs remain broken or malformed. Time conditioning improves generation, but this run does not produce ten clean digits.</p>
</section>
<section id="part-b-2-4"><h3>2.4 Adding class-conditioning to UNet</h3>
<p>I represented each digit label as a ten-element one-hot vector and added two class FCBlocks. At two decoder stages, I use class_embedding × features + time_embedding.</p>
</section>
<section id="part-b-2-5"><h3>2.5 Training the UNet</h3>
<p>During training, I zero the entire class vector with probability 0.1 before embedding it, so the same model can predict an unconditional velocity.</p>
<p>I trained a fresh D = 64 model for ten epochs with batch size 64, Adam initial learning rate 0.01, and the same exponential decay.</p>
<figure class=""><a href="/images/cs180/project-3/b/class_fm_loss.png"><img src="/images/cs180/project-3/b/class_fm_loss.png" width="960" height="360" loading="lazy" alt="Class-conditioned flow-matching training loss with exponential learning rate decay."></a><figcaption>Class-conditioned flow-matching training loss with exponential learning rate decay.</figcaption></figure>
</section>
<section id="part-b-2-6"><h3>2.6 Sampling from the UNet</h3>
<p>Sampling uses 300 Euler steps, h = 1/300, and v = vᵤ + 5(v꜀ − vᵤ). This Part B guidance convention differs from Part A’s v꜀ + w(v꜀ − vᵤ). Each grid requests digits 0–9 across four rows, with sampling seed 180 held fixed across epochs.</p>
<figure class=""><a href="/images/cs180/project-3/b/class_fm_samples_epoch_1.png"><img src="/images/cs180/project-3/b/class_fm_samples_epoch_1.png" width="1440" height="552" loading="lazy" alt="Class-conditioned samples at epoch 1: four instances of each requested digit, 0–9 across every row."></a><figcaption>Class-conditioned samples at epoch 1: four instances of each requested digit, 0–9 across every row.</figcaption></figure>
<figure class=""><a href="/images/cs180/project-3/b/class_fm_samples_epoch_5.png"><img src="/images/cs180/project-3/b/class_fm_samples_epoch_5.png" width="1440" height="552" loading="lazy" alt="Class-conditioned samples at epoch 5: four instances of each requested digit, 0–9 across every row."></a><figcaption>Class-conditioned samples at epoch 5: four instances of each requested digit, 0–9 across every row.</figcaption></figure>
<figure class=""><a href="/images/cs180/project-3/b/class_fm_samples_epoch_10.png"><img src="/images/cs180/project-3/b/class_fm_samples_epoch_10.png" width="1440" height="552" loading="lazy" alt="Class-conditioned samples at epoch 10: four instances of each requested digit, 0–9 across every row."></a><figcaption>Class-conditioned samples at epoch 10: four instances of each requested digit, 0–9 across every row.</figcaption></figure>
<p>Epoch 1 already follows many requested labels, but several digits are rough or fragmented. By epochs 5 and 10, the requested digits are recognizable, with variation in tilt, stroke width, and loop shape. Class conditioning gives more consistent digit shapes than the time-only model in these samples.</p>
<h4 id="part-b-2-6-scheduler">Removing the learning rate scheduler</h4>
<p>I trained an independent model with a constant learning rate of 0.001. I lowered the initial rate from 0.01 to compensate for removing decay; 0.001 is also the scheduled run’s rate after ten decay steps. I kept the architecture, epochs, batch size, class dropout, and sampling settings the same. Both experiments reset the training seed and data-loader seed to 180, and use the same initial sampling noise.</p>
<figure class=""><a href="/images/cs180/project-3/b/class_fm_no_scheduler_loss.png"><img src="/images/cs180/project-3/b/class_fm_no_scheduler_loss.png" width="960" height="360" loading="lazy" alt="Class-conditioned training loss with constant learning rate 0.001."></a><figcaption>Class-conditioned training loss with constant learning rate 0.001.</figcaption></figure>
<figure class=""><a href="/images/cs180/project-3/b/class_fm_no_scheduler_samples_epoch_1.png"><img src="/images/cs180/project-3/b/class_fm_no_scheduler_samples_epoch_1.png" width="1440" height="552" loading="lazy" alt="Constant learning rate 0.001, epoch 1: four instances of each digit, sampling seed 180."></a><figcaption>Constant learning rate 0.001, epoch 1: four instances of each digit, sampling seed 180.</figcaption></figure>
<figure class=""><a href="/images/cs180/project-3/b/class_fm_no_scheduler_samples_epoch_5.png"><img src="/images/cs180/project-3/b/class_fm_no_scheduler_samples_epoch_5.png" width="1440" height="552" loading="lazy" alt="Constant learning rate 0.001, epoch 5: four instances of each digit, sampling seed 180."></a><figcaption>Constant learning rate 0.001, epoch 5: four instances of each digit, sampling seed 180.</figcaption></figure>
<figure class=""><a href="/images/cs180/project-3/b/class_fm_no_scheduler_samples_epoch_10.png"><img src="/images/cs180/project-3/b/class_fm_no_scheduler_samples_epoch_10.png" width="1440" height="552" loading="lazy" alt="Constant learning rate 0.001, epoch 10: four instances of each digit, sampling seed 180."></a><figcaption>Constant learning rate 0.001, epoch 10: four instances of each digit, sampling seed 180.</figcaption></figure>
<table><caption>Mean training velocity MSE</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Epoch</th><th scope="col">Exponential decay</th><th scope="col">Constant 0.001</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">1</th><td>0.169910</td><td>0.136794</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">5</th><td>0.093312</td><td>0.091006</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">10</th><td>0.084713</td><td>0.085003</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>The constant-rate model looks cleaner at epoch 1 and has a lower mean training loss. By epoch 10, both produce recognizable requested digits and their mean losses are close. In this experiment, reducing the constant learning rate maintained similar final performance without a scheduler.</p>
</section>
